<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- THE KEY WITHOUT WHICH THE LAUNCHER CANNOT FIND ANYTHING.
         macOS grants the local network per application, and the bundled
         Python is a child of this app, so everything it sends is attributed
         here. Without this string macOS shows no prompt at all: it simply
         answers "No route to host" to every probe, for ever, which reads
         exactly like a Tiiny that is switched off.

         The words below are what the person sees in the prompt, so they say
         what the app is about to do rather than naming a protocol. -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>The Tiiny App Farm looks for your Tiiny on the USB cable and on your network, and the apps it runs talk to the Tiiny the same way.</string>

    <!-- NSBonjourServices is deliberately absent. It is only needed to browse
         or advertise an mDNS service, and the farm does neither: it reads its
         own side of each USB cable and works out the box's address by
         arithmetic, sends one UDP discovery packet on the network, and asks
         the TiinyOS client at a fixed host name. Declaring services nobody
         browses would ask for access nothing uses. -->
</dict>
</plist>
